  "issue": "The contiguous_block_by_logarithm split, as implemented in code/splits.py, assigns the held-out slice to the topmost contiguous block of logarithm values k (the highest 30% of k in [0,N)). Because the target is MSB(k) = [k < N/2], this block is confounded with the target: verified empirically, the held-out label balance under this split is 0.0 (100% label=0) for BOTH curve_1 and curve_2 (train-split label balance is 0.833). A held-out set with a single class makes '0.5 = chance' an incorrect baseline (a trivial always-predict-0 classifier scores heldout_accuracy=1.0, heldout_advantage=0.5, on ANY dataset regardless of learned content) -- this is visible directly in the raw data as the exact-zero-variance, exact-zero-or-saturated gaps at higher capacities under contiguous_block_by_logarithm. C2 (shuffled labels) was run ONLY on random_by_logarithm and did not surface this, since C2's blocking check is about label-vs-input decoupling, not about split-induced class imbalance.",
  "classification": "invalid_measurement",
